WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:19.808
The American Chestnut tree
was one of the most abundant

00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:20.840
and dominant trees

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.423
in the Eastern United
States Appalachian chain.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.600
They were gorgeous trees.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.797
It is actually the tree that
inspired the Christmas song

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:33.380
"Chestnuts Roasting On An Open Fire".

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.063
It was a very heavily
used resource by people.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.110
In the late 1800s,

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.670
people brought Asian
chestnuts to North America

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.510
as an ornamental tree, and they
carried with them a disease,

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.740
a fungal blight and it infected

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.600
native American Chestnut trees

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.290
and just wiped them out completely.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.733
Over 99% of the population
was obliterated.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.900
Fast forward nearly 100 years

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.860
and a team led by William Powell

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.940
at the State University of New York

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.910
was able to actually
discover why the blight

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.413
kills the Chestnut tree.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.240
And they have produced a
strain of Chestnut trees,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.640
that is 100% resistant
to this fungal blight.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.640
It really shows that there's
no point of no return.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.310
There's potentially no point
you can get where you can't

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.775
end up recovering and healing
the damage that's happened.

00:01:31.775 --> 00:01:33.540
And that's the hope that biotechnology

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.945
brings to conservation.

00:01:39.526 --> 00:01:42.730
The UN is issuing a dire
warning on climate change.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.990
Nearly a million species
are at risk of extinction

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:46.990
because of humans.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:51.530
We are doing irreparable
harm to Earth's biodiversity.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.310
And what is clear is that we're driving

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.363
species to extinction.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.740
The human species has driven

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:04.650
the planet's biodiversity
into an unprecedented decline.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.740
The extinction rate is 1000 times higher

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.410
than it naturally would be

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.870
with as many as one
million species at risk.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:17.823
Many scientists say we are
in a sixth mass extinction.

00:02:17.823 --> 00:02:21.610
The decline in biodiversity
is a crucial issue

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.850
that is largely overlooked
in the public consciousness

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.800
in terms of how important it really is.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.500
Our existence as humans
is deeply intertwined

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.810
with the health and flourishing

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.990
of a number of non-human species.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.260
If you destroy the environment,

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.170
it's going to turn around and bite back.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:44.910
We need to radically
rethink our relationship

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:45.830
to the natural world.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:48.860
We need to understand that
we are a deeply entrenched

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.210
part of it, certainly
not separate from it.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.330
This is part of what is
killing other species,

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.833
and eventually us too.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:59.030
Traditional conservation methods alone

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.560
are struggling to offset
the speed at which

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.170
we're losing species and habitats.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.520
Biotechnologies that have
been developing in the wings

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:12.300
are now offering the hope of
new, if not controversial,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.893
ways of saving endangered species.

00:03:16.245 --> 00:03:21.203
Genetic rescue traditionally
has been the practice

00:03:21.203 --> 00:03:25.341
of increasing a population's
genetic diversity,

00:03:25.341 --> 00:03:27.820
in a way to benefit it.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:29.760
Usually when the population is exhibiting

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.460
some type of problem, like
inbreeding or whatnot.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.030
We're trying to increase genetic diversity

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.950
or increase the viability of
the genetics of a population.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.570
Then in that kind of very core definition,

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:47.920
biotechnologies offer a whole
host of ways to do that.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.090
Biotechnology offers
everything from genetic insight

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.910
to restoring diversity with cloning

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.130
or technologies or doing gene editing,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:00.600
to aid disease resistance,
all the way to that moonshot

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.150
of using all those technologies
to restore something

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.740
like a wooly mammoth
or a passenger pigeon.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.680
The process of resurrecting
species like this

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.831
is known as de-extinction

00:04:10.831 --> 00:04:12.970
and it's one of the more complex

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.570
and controversial applications
of this biotechnology.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.052
Because of biotechnology,

00:04:18.052 --> 00:04:20.450
we can bring back those kinds of animals.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.590
But of course it is a tedious work to do

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.243
but it is not impossible.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.440
De-extinction is a scientific
movement we could say,

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.570
an emerging space of researchers

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:38.110
who are trying to use a variety
of different biotechnologies

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:42.230
in order to help recreate

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:46.200
close approximate versions
of extinct species

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.350
so that they can create new animals

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.480
that mimic extinct species
and can go back out

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.780
into the wild spaces where
extinct species used to roam,

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.500
and carry out important
ecological roles there

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.700
that have disappeared since a
certain species went extinct.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.890
The very first example of de-extinction,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.800
if you call it that, is the bucardo,

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.970
which is a Pyrenean Ibex
or kind of a mountain goat.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:12.900
The last bucardo died in
Spain, it was a single animal,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.490
but they took some cells from
its ear and preserved those.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:19.770
And years later, they used those cells

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.770
to clone a bucardo using a
domestic goat as the surrogate.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.787
So they could create cells,
implant an embryo in the goat

00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:34.380
and the goat gave birth to a live Bucardo.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.210
The species was brought back,

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.620
only to go extinct again
just 10 minutes later,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:41.920
due to lung defects.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.480
The 2003 resurrection of the
bucardo remains the closest

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.840
that anyone has gotten
to true de-extinction,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.083
at least for now.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:57.300
In 2017, George Church's
wooly mammoth project

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.470
captured the public's imagination

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:02.960
and brought de-extinction
back into conversation.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.110
So de-extinction is
really just a little bit

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.400
of hybridization, using ancient DNA

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.210
and some modern synthetic
DNA to achieve a goal

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:17.880
of say cold resistance
or pathogen resistance.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.900
Church hopes to adapt the genome

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.510
of the wooly mammoths
closest living relative,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:26.650
the Asian elephant, to include
a number of mammoth traits.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.660
In particular, how to
thrive in the cold climate

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.240
of the Arctic.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.520
We are using the tools of paleo genomics,

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.990
which is the ability to
actually get DNA sequences

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:41.510
from those extinct species from
100s or 1000s of years ago,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.360
and look at their genetic code,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.220
compare it to their living relatives

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.780
and start to understand what
are the unique fragments

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.240
of DNA that made them do what they did

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.364
in the environment.

00:06:52.364 --> 00:06:55.384
Two, there's now something
called gene editing,

00:06:55.384 --> 00:06:59.060
which is most famously
done with CRISPR Cas-9.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.740
It's an enzyme and RNA combination,

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.210
that basically is a homing
system that allows scientists

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:11.690
to target any region of DNA
in a living cells genome

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:15.580
and make a cut and then
make an edit to that area.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:19.020
So we could take the hemoglobin gene

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.970
in an Asian elephants genome,

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:26.290
and cut it and overwrite
in its place the hemoglobin

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:29.410
a wheel from a wooly
mammoth, which will change

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:32.360
how it bonds oxygen at
different temperatures.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:33.820
And that's something
that George Church's lab

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.930
has already done in
culturing a Petri dish.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.330
So it used to be a big deal
to make one precise change

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:41.210
in the genome of an animal.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.690
We've made 42 changes in pigs.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.580
We now have 2000 adult pigs

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:49.230
that are called three point, version 3.0,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.900
that are used for organ donations.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.590
And that's being tested
now in preclinical trials.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.330
That shows we can do
42 in a cell and a lab,

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.650
and then move the nucleus into an egg

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.963
and bring it to term all
the way to adulthood.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:08.863
Thousands of times.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.380
So we wanna do the same
thing in elephants,

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:13.440
which are a little bit bigger than pigs,

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.160
little slower but otherwise it should be

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.423
a very similar procedure.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.998
While altering the
genome of living animals

00:08:21.998 --> 00:08:25.720
like the Asian elephant
could help bring a version

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.060
of extinct species back to life,

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.960
it could also help to rescue a species

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:32.950
which is itself endangered.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:35.920
We want something that
has all the advantages

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:41.810
of cold resistance, possibly
resistance to pathogens

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:46.810
like the EEHV virus, that's
almost extinction level

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.320
harm to the Asian elephant,
which is an endangered species.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.380
So you can consider it a hybrid

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.780
but it also, we can
use synthetic biologies

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:00.780
to say alter tusk length
to avoid poachers.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:02.945
So there's a number of opportunities here

00:09:02.945 --> 00:09:06.960
that is not limited to
de-extinction of genes.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.280
Church wants to re-introduce his hybrids

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.900
into the Arctic Tundra,
which he hopes will preserve

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.680
an environment that's
crucial to storing carbon.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.660
Science has proven
de-extinction is possible,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:23.200
and the environmental argument
for doing it is clear,

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.710
yet questions still remain.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.510
Doing de-extinction, there's
a whole lot of unknowns.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:31.810
And especially when you're
trying to think about a species

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:32.730
like a wooly mammoth

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.230
that's been gone for thousands of years.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:38.420
What are the prospects for
being able to put this back

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.080
in the appropriate kind of environment.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.233
Does wooly mammoth habitat
even exist anymore?

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:47.240
And if you go for very long extinctions,

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.080
the further back you go,
the more uncertainty,

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.730
the less we know about the species

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:53.543
and the less likely there
is to be a place for it now.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.740
The technical ability is
outrunning our ability

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:02.470
to think about is this a
sensible or a useful thing to do.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.210
And this is concern that, you know,

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.540
if we're dealing with
something as fundamental

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.340
as the genetic makeup of species

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:12.163
and thinking about putting
them back out into the wild,

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.890
you know, the genie's
out of the bottle a bit.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:17.240
What does it mean to take the tools

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.030
from synthetic biology and merge them

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:21.780
with the more traditional
conservation biologists,

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:24.370
who are losing the game,
as we're, you know,

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:26.140
also losing more species,

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:29.450
can really fruitful combination be born

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:32.210
from this hybridization of knowledge sets.

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.130
And indeed it seems to be happening.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.410
There are interesting projects
getting off the ground,

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:37.730
when you bring these two different ways

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:41.070
of looking at nature together,
something that's engineerable

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.770
and something that's worth saving.

